# Era 9 — Data Persistence (V29–V31)

_Phase 2 · Concept Layer V1_

**Status: ✅ done, verified 2026-09-17**

**Implementation note:** all three pages written new — `concepts/persistent-storage.mdx`
(V29, highest-stakes accuracy requirement — the destructive-action warning matches
`cli/config.mdx`'s own verified volume/bind behavior exactly), `concepts/backups-and-restoration.mdx`
(V30), `concepts/connecting-to-services-locally.mdx` (V31). The reference-target question
Era 1 left open (gap 001, now gap 001 in this file's own log) is resolved: `beaver service
connect` (source-verified against `commands/service/connect.go` via `cli/service.mdx`'s
already-accurate coverage) is the correct target.

Covers T22–T24: Persistent storage, Backups and restoration, Connecting to services locally.

---

## V29 — Persistent storage (T22)

### Goal
New concept page: volumes vs. bind mounts, external storage requirements, placement
restrictions, what survives deletion, which actions permanently destroy data.

### Reuses
Whichever `cli/*.mdx` page(s) cover volumes (likely under `config` per the sibling
roadmap's inventory noting `config` includes a `volume` subgroup) as reference target.
Links to Containers and replicas (V20) for "what survives a restart or replacement," which
that page already states — cross-link, don't repeat.

### Implement
`concepts/persistent-storage.mdx`: volume-vs-bind-mount as a clear either/or with when to
use each, "placement restrictions" stated as user-facing constraints (e.g., a volume may tie
a workload to a specific machine — stated as a consequence, not why the constraint exists),
"which actions permanently destroy data" as an explicit, prominent list — a strong
`<Warning>` candidate per `components.txt`, since this is exactly the kind of irreversible
consequence Invariant 4 (of the master architecture doc) requires stating plainly.

### Validate
Every "permanently destroys data" claim verified against actual behavior (Invariant 1) —
this is the single highest-stakes accuracy requirement in the whole roadmap; an
under-stated destructive action is a support/trust incident, not just a doc gap.

### Done when
Page exists; destructive-action list independently verified, not assumed from memory of how
similar systems work.

---

## V30 — Backups and restoration (T23)

### Goal
New concept page: manual/scheduled backups, retention, destinations, backup status,
restoring into a new service, restore compatibility, verifying recovered data.

### Reuses
`cli/service.mdx`'s `backup-destination` subgroup as reference target.

### Implement
`concepts/backups-and-restoration.mdx`: retention and scheduling as user-configured settings
with stated defaults, "restore compatibility" as an explicit constraint list (what you can
and can't restore into), "verifying recovered data" as a stated post-restore step users
should take — this closes the loop the topic itself names (a restore isn't done until
verified). Checkpoint term kept out: `backup encoding/format internals` (from
`DISCLOSURE-ADDENDUM.md`).

### Validate
Disclosure pass on encoding/format language; cross-check restore-compatibility claims
against actual supported paths (Invariant 1).

### Done when
Page exists; passes disclosure pass.

---

## V31 — Connecting to services locally (T24)

### Goal
New concept page: supported connection/tunnel workflows, prerequisites, connection
lifetime, local client examples, troubleshooting connectivity.

### Reuses
Whichever `cli/*.mdx` covers local service connections/tunneling (check the sibling
roadmap's inventory for the exact command family at write time — not yet fixed here) as
reference target.

### Implement
`concepts/connecting-to-services-locally.mdx`: connection lifetime stated as an observable
property (how long a tunnel stays open, what ends it), local client examples using the
shared fictional vocabulary (Era 2 V4) so this page's examples match every other page's,
troubleshooting section linking to Troubleshooting (T33, Era 11) for the general
symptom-based flow rather than duplicating it here.

### Validate
Confirms the reference-target command family exists and is named correctly (a small
verification step given this target wasn't fixed in Era 1's cross-reference).

### Done when
Page exists; reference link resolves to a real, correctly-named target; closes Era 9.
